                            Section("Incremental fit",
                                    [Prose("Some statistics have an exact update, in which the new value follows from a running state and one new observation. An estimator of such a statistic folds each new observation into its state, and it returns the statistic from the state without reading the sample again. The state is in the estimator's `cache` field. The sample mean, the sample variance and the full-moment sample covariance have an exact update. So do the `FullMoment` forms of [`Coskewness`](@ref) and [`Cokurtosis`](@ref). The `SemiMoment` forms do not, because they clip each observation against a centre, and a new observation moves the centre."),
                                     Prose("Two functions fold an observation into the state. Each family of estimators implements [`partial_fit!`](@ref) as its fastest exact update, and the call can change the estimator you pass in. [`partial_fit`](@ref) is one generic method that folds a copy of the state, so the estimator you pass in keeps its old value."),
                                     Cap(:partial_fit!), Cap(:partial_fit),
                                     Prose("An estimator of a statistic with no exact update keeps the observations it has seen instead, and it runs the batch computation over them to return the statistic. [`Online`](@ref) gives the estimator that store of observations. It applies once, at the warm-up before the first fold, and no `Online` wrapper remains in the estimator after that. Its `max_history` sets the largest number of observations the store keeps. This limits the memory, and it changes the result of any estimator that reads the stored observations. A statistic with an exact update does not change, and it stays fitted over every observation so far."),
                                     Cap(:Online)]),
                            Section("Coverage policy",
