                                         [Cap(:IdentityScaling),
                                          Cap(:IdiosyncraticVarianceScaling)]),
                                   Group("Rules that compute the radius of the covariance set from the sample and the factors, in place of a number you state",
                                         [Cap(:ResidualInflation), Cap(:VarianceFraction)])]),
                            Group("Axis tags of the ellipsoid and the norm ball",
                                  [Cap(:MuUncertaintySetClass),
                                   Cap(:SigmaUncertaintySetClass)])]),
                   Section("Turnover",
                           [Prose("The turnover is the absolute difference, asset by asset, between the current weights and a vector of benchmark weights. You can use it as a constraint, as the base of a fee, or as a risk measure. [`turnover_constraints`](@ref), [`TurnoverEstimator`](@ref) and [`Turnover`](@ref) implement it.")]),
                   Section("Fees",
                           [Prose("Every optimisation except a finite allocation and `BudgetedHindsightPath` can include fees, the naive ones too. [`calc_fees`](@ref) and [`calc_asset_fees`](@ref) compute the fees of a portfolio and of each asset, which you can subtract from the expected returns. The proportional and turnover rates apply in every period, and the two fixed amounts apply one time for the whole holding period. [`calc_total_fees`](@ref) and [`calc_total_asset_fees`](@ref) therefore give the cost over a horizon you state."),
                            Cap(:fees_constraints),
                            Cap(:calc_fixed_fees, :calc_asset_fixed_fees),
                            Cap(:calc_total_fees, :calc_total_asset_fees),
                            Group(Cap(:FeesEstimator, :Fees),
                                  [Note("Proportional long"), Note("Proportional short"),
                                   Note("Fixed long"), Note("Fixed short"),
                                   Note("Turnover")]), Cap(:AmortisedFees),
                            Cap(:FirstObservationFees)]),
                   Section("Portfolio returns and drawdowns",
                           [Prose("Many risk measures and analyses need the returns, the cumulative returns and the drawdowns of a portfolio, in total and per asset. [`calc_net_returns`](@ref), [`calc_net_asset_returns`](@ref), [`cumulative_returns`](@ref) and [`drawdowns`](@ref) compute them. [`calc_turnover`](@ref) computes the trading that a path of weights needs, which is the value of the quantity that [`Turnover`](@ref) bounds."),
                            Prose("You can also score a window on the weights a fund holds, which move with the returns of their assets while no trade happens. The series is then the wealth ratio of those drifted holdings."),
                            Prose("The realised series of a fold can also charge the two fixed fees on a schedule of its own. The scheme states that schedule in its `fa` field. It replaces the schedule of the fee itself, and it has no effect on the fit."),
                            Cap(:SelfFinancingDrift)]),
                   Section("[Tracking](@id catalogue-tracking)",
                           [Prose("A tracking constraint keeps a portfolio close to a benchmark, which can be an index, an indicator or another portfolio."),
                            Group(Cap(:tracking_benchmark, :TrackingError),
                                  [Cap(:ReturnsTracking), Cap(:WeightsTracking)]),
                            Prose("[`norm_error`](@ref) computes the error with one of the norms below."),
                            Group("Norm tracking algorithms",
                                  [Cap(:L1Norm), Cap(:L2Norm), Cap(:SquaredL2Norm),
                                   Cap(:LpNorm), Cap(:LInfNorm)]),
                            Prose("The distance can also be a difference of risk, measured against a [`WeightsTracking`](@ref) benchmark, in place of a norm of the difference of returns. Two methods compute it."),
                            Group(Cap(:RiskTrackingError),
                                  [Cap(:DependentVariableTracking),
                                   Cap(:IndependentVariableTracking)])]),
                   Section("Risk measures",
                           [Prose("The sections below group the risk measures by the optimisations that accept them."),
                            Prose("The fields of a risk measure that come from the prior, `mu`, `sigma`, `kt` and `sk`, take either the value or the estimator that computes it, a [`DeferredQuantity`](@ref). The optimisation evaluates the estimator on its own prior, so the value is refitted in each cross-validation fold and in each subset of a meta optimiser. A matrix you pass in stays the same. A measure with two or more such fields can instead name one prior estimator in `pe`, and one fit then fills every field you leave empty."),
                            Section("Calibration",
                                    [Prose("A tail probability and a deformation parameter also take a rule in place of the number. The field fixes the quantity and the end of the distribution, so you write only the rule. The optimisation evaluates the rule on its own prior, as it does a deferred estimator. The type of each field accepts the one family of rules that computes its quantity. It takes a callable estimator of that family, a plain function of `(key, pr, w, slv, ctx)`, or the number. Five rules ship. Two compute a significance level, and three compute a deformation parameter. Forty fields over twenty-seven risk measures and weight builders take a rule, and they are every `alpha` and `beta` and every `kappa`, `kappa_a` and `kappa_b`. The two inner integration bounds of the tail Gini family, `alpha_i` and `beta_i`, are starting points and not quantities to estimate. They keep their numbers, and each fold checks the bound `0 < alpha_i < alpha < 1` against the calibrated `alpha`."),
                                     Prose("A significance rule reads the length of the sample. Where the count it states is a count of observations, it reads the effective weights of the observations. A deformation rule reads the probability of its own end of the distribution, which it gets in the `CalibrationContext` that the owner of the field builds. One deformation rule spends a stated entropy budget on the length of the sample. The other two estimate the tail index of the sample and return its reciprocal. One of them standardises each column and returns a value per end, so a skewed sample gives two numbers. The other whitens each observation with the covariance matrix and returns one number for both ends."),
                                     Prose("A rule that reads the shape of a series needs to know which series. A drawdown measure prices the drawdown series of the portfolio, not its returns, and the key of the field does not say which. The measure therefore states its series in the `CalibrationContext`, beside the significance level. The two tail rules then read the drawdown series of each column in place of the column itself. The pooled rule pools those series. The radial rule whitens their rows with the covariance matrix of the same drawdown series, because a prior result holds no moment of the drawdowns. The series belongs to the measure, and no rule holds it. If you run a rule by hand, build the context with a `series`, such as `AbsoluteDrawdownSeries()`, as the measure would."),
                                     Cap(:ScenarioCount), Cap(:RateSignificance),
                                     Cap(:EntropyBudget), Cap(:HillTailDecay),
                                     Cap(:RadialTailDecay), Cap(:ReturnsSeries),
                                     Cap(:AbsoluteDrawdownSeries),
                                     Cap(:RelativeDrawdownSeries),
                                     Prose("An ambiguity radius also takes a rule. A radius applies to no end of the distribution, so one type bound serves every radius field. The fields are the four regularisation coefficients of [`JuMPOptimiser`](@ref) and the radii of the distributionally robust risk measures. Four rules ship, and all four compute a radius. Two shrink the ball at the rate of the square root of the sample length. The third shrinks it at a rate that the number of assets sets, which is much slower over a wide universe."),
                                     Cap(:ConcentrationRadius), Cap(:RateRadius),
                                     Cap(:DimensionalRateRadius),
                                     Prose("Those three return the same number for every field, but the eight radius fields do not all measure distance in one norm. The fourth rule reads the key of the field, picks the ground metric that the field names, and returns the sampling error of the empirical measure in that metric. As a result, the `l1` and `linf` coefficients of one optimiser get two different values."),
                                     Cap(:DualNormRadius),
                                     Prose("The tail weight of an Esfahani-Kuhn loss has a family of its own. One rule ships for it, and it sets the tail term of the loss to a stated multiple of its mean term. A tail weight you state has no unit, but its effect depends on the scale of the sample, so one number gives a different trade-off at each sampling frequency. The rule reads the probability of its own field, which it gets in the same context."),
                                     Cap(:TailTermParity),
                                     Prose("A norm ceiling is a different quantity from a radius and has a family of its own. Neither family is accepted in the field of the other. A radius is the coefficient of a norm penalty in the objective. A ceiling bounds that norm in a constraint. The bound sets a floor on the effective number of assets. The three norm-constraint fields of [`JuMPOptimiser`](@ref), `l2c`, `lpc` and `linfc`, take one. One rule ships. It keeps a stated fraction of the assets effective, so the floor changes with the assets of the prior. The order of the norm belongs to the constraint and not to the rule, so each constraint gives the rule its own order before the field is evaluated."),
                                     Cap(:EffectiveAssetFloor)]),
